debate/steps/step-004/r1/grok.md:1:I'll start by reading the full prompt, the relevant methodology skills, and the Julius evidence so CALON-G is specified from locked numbers rather than invented ones.I’ll now pull the frozen CALON-F spec, Lp(a) policy, comparator PDFs, and prior debate artefacts so the CALON-G formula is locked against those, not against the withdrawn tally.I have the locked Julius numbers. Next I’ll inspect CALON-F, the grey-zone lipid term, Lp(a) units, and the comparator PDFs so the primary formula is a pre-specification, not a post-hoc win hunt.I have the locked Julius numbers, the CALON-F defects (especially `sp50` and the invalid horizon mask), and the inventory column names. Drafting the Cycle-2 protocol next — one pre-specified formula, no win claim.# Step 004 · Round 1 · grok
debate/steps/step-004/r1/grok.md:26:“Aim to be competitive” means: cover the clinical domains the published scores already use, add the one lipid-burden construct that is this programme’s identity, freeze the formula, then fit once. It does **not** mean: inspect the withdrawn subgroup matrix, add BMI where diabetics lost, or promote a grey-zone enhancer because `outputs/grey_zone_enhancers.json` looked interesting. Cycle-1 already did that with BMI (`code/15_CALON_FINAL.py` lines 85–96) and had to walk it back.
debate/steps/step-004/r1/grok.md:71:| Grey-zone apoB/HDL | **Go from primary** | A 5–20% two-stage enhancer is a subgroup-conditional model. Promoting it after `grey_zone_enhancers.json` (“no gain”, delta C +0.015, CI crosses 0) would be Cycle-1 BMI again. Constraint 1 allows it only as the **single** sensitivity, and only as a **main-effect substitution**, not as a grey-zone procedure. |
debate/steps/step-004/r1/grok.md:72:| Lp(a) | **Stay, native nmol/L only** | A 2026 FH-adjacent risk model that silently omits Lp(a) will be asked why. SAFEHEART Table 3 multivariable HR 1.52 for Lp(a)>50 mg/dL; FH-RS carries Lp(a)≥50 mg/dL. Prior “HR 1.033, no gain” is from the grey-zone script on n=3,333 with the old risk set — it is not a licence to drop a pre-specified causal marker after peeking. **Unit policy is frozen below.** Missingness is handled in-fold; it is not a reason to delete the term. |
debate/steps/step-004/r-implement/claude.md:63:| `02_calong_fit.R` | Design build with provenance, age-spike screen, collinearity report, missingness profile, ridge Cox with nested-CV λ, OOF LP, Schoenfeld PH, frozen coefficients, grey sensitivity |
debate/steps/step-000/PROMPT_R2.md:534:**Does it change practice?** No, and the file says so. Step-002 §D.3: grey-zone AUC **0.489** on n = 234 / 22 events in the 5–10% band. `STATUS.md` on `code/17_grey_zone_enhancers.py`: log(apoB/LDL-C) HR 1.154 (1.027, 1.295) per SD but ΔC **+0.0146 (−0.0050, +0.0330)** — interval covers zero. And in `calon_final.json`, the subgroups where a clinician most needs help are where the model is weakest: `on statin` C = **0.612** (131 events), `diabetes` C = **0.559** (64 events), Welsh `hypertensive` C = **0.558** (52 events). A score at 0.56 in diabetics is not usable for triage. This is a marker, not a tool — `CLAUDE.md` §3 already uses exactly that phrase about the discordance chapter, and it applies here.
debate/steps/step-000/PROMPT_R2.md:545:**What can honestly be claimed today?** That in a cohort of UK Biobank LDLR variant carriers and an All-Wales FH registry, a pre-specified seven-variable model achieves cross-validated C of 0.70 and 0.75 respectively, with performance falling to 0.56–0.61 in treated and diabetic strata and no discrimination in the 5–10% grey zone; and that it was **not distinguishable** from three published FH scores in the Welsh registry across all 30 estimable cells. The UK Biobank comparator results should be held back entirely until §2a is resolved. `STATUS.md`'s own standing rule — "Concede the weak link first" (`CLAUDE.md` §4) — points the same way.
debate/steps/step-000/PROMPT_R2.md:759:- The grey-zone point estimate of AUC 0.489 with 22 events. Without a confidence interval it is not proof of worse-than-chance performance, but it provides no evidence of useful discrimination where treatment decisions are difficult.
debate/steps/step-000/PROMPT_R2.md:889:  **The model is near-chance exactly where decisions bite:** Welsh hypertensives C 0.5585 (52 events); UKB diabetics C 0.5593 (64 events, the one declared loss); grey-zone 5–10% band AUC 0.489 (brief D3) — below chance in the band where a score must earn its keep — and both enhancers null (STATUS.md grey-zone section: apoB/LDL-C ΔC +0.0146 [−0.0050, +0.0330], Lp(a) HR 1.033).
debate/steps/step-000/r2/kimi.txt:69:  **Where I converge with all three agents on the clinical floor:** phenotype (+0.226 mmol/L vs +3-4), external parity (Wales 0 WIN / 30 tie / 0 LOSS, verified in the JSON), the model weakest where triage bites (UKB diabetes C = 0.5593, the single LOSS at −0.0644/64 events; Welsh hypertensive C = 0.5585; grey-zone AUC 0.489), and a coronary-weighted endpoint sold as ASCVD. Nothing in Round 1 or in my disk checks this round moves any of those.
